From 77122b731e1a446bce0e8e1b461863f96235443b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 21:58:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] registry: our DSpark verdict was never ours -- the drafter
 cannot load on this fork

The row said DSpark "engaged on 780M Vulkan but fell from 7.89 to 4.27 t/s
(0.54x)" and was therefore OFF. That measurement is the PrismML fork's binary.
On THIS fork the drafter does not load at all, so we have been publishing
somebody else's number as our own conclusion.

Two blockers, both pinned rather than described.

The file is refused by gguf_init: 'dspark.fc.weight' has offset 337718592,
expected 357584192. That is F-10, and the arithmetic isolates it to one tensor
-- token_embd.weight declares type-id 42 (18 bytes per 64 values) while the file
leaves it 337,715,200 bytes for 1,271,398,400 elements, which is 0.265625
bytes/element, which is 34 bytes per 128, which is type-id 43 exactly. Payload
right, declaration wrong. A four-byte retag on a COPY clears it.

Then the real one: unknown model architecture 'dspark'. LLM_ARCH_DFLASH is
registered and wired through llama-arch, llama-context and llama-model;
"dspark" appears nowhere in src/. But llama-arch DOES define the DSpark tensors
and common/speculative.cpp carries the entire driver. So the fork holds DSpark's
driver and tensor names without its model architecture, and the --spec-type
draft-dspark flag is unreachable in practice. That is what "merged inside the
rebase window, never tested" actually meant: the speculative half merged, the
architecture half did not.

So whether DSpark helps here is UNKNOWN, not settled, and the 0.54x must stop
being quoted as ours.

A third thing, independent of both: DSpark's confidence-scheduled verification
is gated behind params.p_min > 0.0f and p_min defaults to 0.0, so the command
this registry records ran the drafter with its own scheduler switched off. Any
future measurement sweeps p_min.

The trap for whoever ports it is named in the row: do not shortcut by retagging
general.architecture to dflash. If the Markov head then goes silently unloaded,
the run measures DFlash and reports DSpark, which is worse than no number.

Origin: lane-110G mission 1f, reopened by the President after I had left it
  untouched and called it done.
Measured-effect: no throughput measurement was possible -- that IS the result.
  Evidence is the two load failures, quoted verbatim, plus the offset arithmetic
  in trial-evidence/110/dspark/inspect_drafter.py.
Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md b/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
index f45d17904..1994ce059 100644
--- a/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
+++ b/docs/OPTIONS-REGISTRY.md
@@ -47,7 +47,7 @@ safe measurement posture.
 | KV f16 / q4_0 | f16 primary where capacity permits | KV-cache storage precision. | Use q4_0 only as an explicit context-capacity trade-off. | Project default: f16 is primary; at 64 GB RAM f16 is unconditional for the intended path. Do not claim an unsourced universal speed or quality delta. |
 | `GGML_RECURRENT_STATE_F16=1` | unset (`f32`) | Stores the recurrent GDN/SSM S-state cache in f16; the GDN kernel still computes in f32. | Opt in only for a recurrent/hybrid model after its output and long-context quality gate passes. | Halves S-state residency and read/write traffic. Recurrent rounding can compound, so it stays opt-in pending target-specific greedy and quality validation. |
 | `-cmoe` and `-ngl` | placement-specific | Controls CPU MoE placement and GPU layer offload. | First prefer full unified placement when it fits; use tiered placement when it does not. | Full unified `-ngl 99 --n-cpu-moe 0 -c 4096` reached `29.2` t/s in the recorded fit case. CPU-only streamed was `1.6` t/s; GPU-tiered `-ngl 99 -cmoe` was `3.0` t/s. |
-| DSpark: `--spec-type draft-dspark --spec-draft-n-max 4 -ngld 999` | OFF on current rig | Target-specific speculative drafter for Ternary-Bonsai-27B. | Only after a target-specific controlled benchmark on another hardware path. | It engaged on 780M Vulkan but fell from `7.89` to `4.27` t/s (`0.54×`). `N=4` is required; other values crash. |
+| DSpark: `--spec-type draft-dspark --spec-draft-n-max 4 -ngld 999` | **UNREACHABLE on this fork** — not "off after a bad result" | Target-specific speculative drafter for Ternary-Bonsai-27B. The CLI accepts the flag and `common/speculative.cpp` carries the full driver (`COMMON_SPECULATIVE_TYPE_DRAFT_DSPARK`, the `is_dspark` branches), and `llama-arch.cpp` even defines its tensors (`markov_w1`, `markov_w2`, `conf_proj`). | **You cannot**, today, and that is the finding. | **CORRECTED 2026-07-30 (lane-110G). The prior text — "engaged on 780M Vulkan but fell from 7.89 to 4.27 t/s (0.54×)" — describes a run on the PrismML fork's binary, not on ours, and this registry presented it as our verdict.** On this fork the drafter does not load at all. Two blockers, both pinned:<br>**(1) The file is refused by `gguf_init`** — `tensor 'dspark.fc.weight' has offset 337718592, expected 357584192`. That is F-10: `token_embd.weight` declares type-id 42 (Q2_0, 18 B per 64 values) while the file leaves it 337,715,200 B for 1,271,398,400 elements = 0.265625 B/element = 34 B per 128 = type-id **43**. Payload correct, declaration wrong. A four-byte re-tag on a **copy** clears it (`trial-evidence/110/dspark/retag_drafter.py`; the original is untouched).<br>**(2) Then the architecture is unknown** — `unknown model architecture: 'dspark'`. **There is no `LLM_ARCH_DSPARK` in this tree**: `LLM_ARCH_DFLASH`/`"dflash"` is registered and wired through `llama-arch`, `llama-context` and `llama-model`, and `"dspark"` appears nowhere in `src/`. So the fork carries DSpark's *driver* and *tensor names* but not its *model architecture*, and no DSpark GGUF can be loaded.<br>**Therefore no DSpark throughput number on this fork exists, and the 0.54× must not be quoted as one.** Whether DSpark helps here is **UNKNOWN**, not settled. What is needed is a real port (register the architecture and route it through the DFlash loader plus the three extra tensors), and the trap to avoid is re-tagging the file's `general.architecture` to `dflash` and measuring *that* — if the Markov head is then silently unused, the result would be DFlash reported as DSpark.<br>**Separately, `p_min` matters and was never set.** DSpark's confidence-scheduled verification is gated behind `params.p_min > 0.0f` (`speculative.cpp:1184`) and `p_min` defaults to `0.0` (`common.h:333`), so the recorded command exercised the drafter with its scheduler disabled. `N=4` is required; other values crash. |
 | DSpark iGPU detection correctness | DSpark remains OFF-path on the 780M | In the prerequisite Qwen3.5 GDN rows-mode path, classify `GGML_BACKEND_DEVICE_TYPE_IGPU` as a GPU before validating backend support. | Required whenever that DSpark-capable path is present. | SPINE lane-71 measured the drafter itself at `7.89 -> 4.27` t/s (`0.54x` net loss). Ship this for iGPU-detection correctness, not as a DSpark recommendation on this hardware. |
 | `GGML_SCHED_PREFETCH_EXPERTS` | OFF on unified-memory defaults | thecodacus patch 2: overlaps host-offloaded expert upload with compute. | Discrete-GPU or proven host-offloaded transfer regime. | Documented `+64.5%` prefill in the host-offloaded `-cmoe` condition. Inert when experts are already GPU-resident; it does not enable MTP. |
 | `GGML_SCHED_MAX_PREFETCH_SLOTS` | implementation default | thecodacus patch 3: sizes per-layer prefetch slots. | Tune only with patch 1 and 2 in dependency order and a transfer-bound workload. | No independent isolated gain accepted; part of the host-transfer bundle and inert in the shipped GPU-resident Vulkan configuration. |
